    content: `# Homebuyer FAQ\n\n**Q: How much do I need for a down payment?**\nA: As little as 0% (VA/USDA), 3% (Conventional for qualifying buyers), 3.5% (FHA), or 5%+ (standard Conventional). Down payment assistance may reduce this further.\n\n**Q: What credit score do I need to buy a home?**\nA: 620+ for most conventional loans, 580+ for FHA. Higher scores get better rates.\n\n**Q: How long does the homebuying process take?**\nA: Typically 30-60 days from accepted offer to closing. Pre-approval beforehand takes 1-3 days.\n\n**Q: What is PMI and how do I avoid it?**\nA: Private Mortgage Insurance is required on conventional loans with less than 20% down. Avoid it by putting 20%+ down or using a VA/USDA loan.\n\n**Q: Can I buy a home with student loan debt?**\nA: Yes. Lenders look at your debt-to-income ratio. Student loans are included in DTI calculations.\n\n**Q: What's the difference between pre-qualification and pre-approval?**\nA: Pre-qualification is informal. Pre-approval requires verified documentation and carries real weight with sellers.\n\n**Q: What does a real estate agent cost me as a buyer?**\nA: Typically nothing. Buyer's agent commissions are paid from the seller's proceeds.\n\n**Q: Should I get a home inspection?**\nA: Always. A home inspection (typically $300-$500) can reveal issues worth thousands to negotiate or avoid.\n\n**Q: What is earnest money?**\nA: A good-faith deposit (typically 1-2% of purchase price) submitted with your offer. Applied to your down payment or closing costs at closing.\n\n**Q: Can I use gift funds for a down payment?**\nA: Yes, with a gift letter. FHA, VA, and many conventional loans allow gift funds from family members.\n\n---\n${ADVISOR.name} | NMLS #${ADVISOR.nmls} | ${ADVISOR.email}`
